00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:01,985
The numbers.

64
00:02:02,165 --> 00:02:04,226
What does catching up from your thirties

65
00:02:04,226 --> 00:02:05,102
really look like?

66
00:02:05,452 --> 00:02:06,810
Let's start from zero.

67
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:09,591
Say you're thirty five with nothing invested.

68
00:02:09,771 --> 00:02:12,015
If you invest five hundred dollars a

69
00:02:12,015 --> 00:02:13,449
month until sixty five,

70
00:02:13,449 --> 00:02:16,004
at a seven percent average yearly return,

71
00:02:16,004 --> 00:02:18,249
it could grow to roughly six hundred

72
00:02:18,249 --> 00:02:19,558
ten thousand dollars.

73
00:02:19,738 --> 00:02:22,550
You'd have contributed one hundred eighty thousand

74
00:02:22,550 --> 00:02:23,000
dollars.

75
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,631
Compounding does the rest.

76
00:02:24,982 --> 00:02:27,086
Five hundred dollars a month,

77
00:02:27,086 --> 00:02:29,771
invested at seven percent a year from

78
00:02:29,771 --> 00:02:31,948
age thirty five to sixty five.

79
00:02:32,298 --> 00:02:34,291
Now raise that to seven hundred fifty

80
00:02:34,291 --> 00:02:35,154
dollars a month.

81
00:02:35,334 --> 00:02:36,912
Over the same thirty years,

82
00:02:36,912 --> 00:02:38,958
it could grow to about nine hundred

83
00:02:38,958 --> 00:02:40,419
fifteen thousand dollars.

84
00:02:40,599 --> 00:02:42,880
And at one thousand dollars a month,

85
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:45,161
about one point two million dollars.

86
00:02:45,341 --> 00:02:47,675
From a standing start at thirty five.

87
00:02:48,025 --> 00:02:50,596
Starting at thirty five with nothing saved,

88
00:02:50,596 --> 00:02:53,167
investing for thirty years at seven percent

89
00:02:53,167 --> 00:02:53,586
a year.

90
00:02:53,936 --> 00:02:56,006
And most people in their thirties aren't

91
00:02:56,006 --> 00:02:56,989
starting from zero.

92
00:02:57,169 --> 00:03:00,090
Say you have twenty thousand dollars in

93
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:02,486
an old 401k from a previous job.

94
00:03:02,666 --> 00:03:04,965
Left alone for thirty years at seven

95
00:03:04,965 --> 00:03:07,455
percent, that alone could grow to about

96
00:03:07,455 --> 00:03:09,690
one hundred sixty thousand dollars.

97
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:11,289
Add five hundred a month,

98
00:03:11,289 --> 00:03:13,502
and the total could reach roughly seven

99
00:03:13,502 --> 00:03:14,921
hundred seventy thousand.

100
00:03:15,271 --> 00:03:16,966
What about a five year delay?

101
00:03:17,146 --> 00:03:19,182
If you wait until forty and then

102
00:03:19,182 --> 00:03:21,601
invest five hundred a month for twenty

103
00:03:21,601 --> 00:03:23,892
five years, the total could be about

104
00:03:23,892 --> 00:03:26,120
four hundred five thousand dollars.

105
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:28,413
That five year delay costs around two

106
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:29,841
hundred thousand dollars.

107
00:03:30,021 --> 00:03:30,904
To make it up,

108
00:03:30,904 --> 00:03:33,175
you'd need to invest closer to seven

109
00:03:33,175 --> 00:03:35,257
hundred fifty a month from forty.

110
00:03:35,608 --> 00:03:37,427
So the math in your thirties is

111
00:03:37,427 --> 00:03:37,779
clear.

112
00:03:37,959 --> 00:03:40,757
Starting now matters more than starting perfectly.

113
00:03:40,937 --> 00:03:42,887
And the amount you save each month

114
00:03:42,887 --> 00:03:43,690
matters a lot,

115
00:03:43,690 --> 00:03:45,583
because you have less time than a

116
00:03:45,583 --> 00:03:46,730
twenty two year old.

117
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:49,018
That's why the playbook focuses heavily on

118
00:03:49,018 --> 00:03:50,323
raising your savings rate.

119
00:03:50,674 --> 00:03:52,888
Where do people in their thirties actually

120
00:03:52,888 --> 00:03:53,205
stand?

121
00:03:53,385 --> 00:03:56,409
According to the Federal Reserve's Survey of

122
00:03:56,409 --> 00:03:59,708
Consumer Finances, median net worth for families

123
00:03:59,708 --> 00:04:02,389
headed by someone under thirty five was

124
00:04:02,389 --> 00:04:05,345
about thirty nine thousand dollars in 2022,

125
00:04:05,345 --> 00:04:07,613
and for ages thirty five to forty

126
00:04:07,613 --> 00:04:10,707
four, about one hundred thirty five thousand.

127
00:04:10,887 --> 00:04:12,657
Medians hide huge variation,

128
00:04:12,657 --> 00:04:14,807
but they show that many people are

129
00:04:14,807 --> 00:04:16,703
still building in this decade.

130
00:04:17,054 --> 00:04:20,001
Fidelity suggests a rough guideline of having

131
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:22,425
about one times your salary saved for

132
00:04:22,425 --> 00:04:23,800
retirement by thirty,

133
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:25,831
and about three times by forty.

134
00:04:26,011 --> 00:04:27,224
If you're below that,

135
00:04:27,224 --> 00:04:28,553
you're in good company.

136
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:30,506
These are targets to aim for,

137
00:04:30,506 --> 00:04:31,972
not grades on your life.

138
00:04:32,323 --> 00:04:34,041
Now, the cost side.

139
00:04:34,221 --> 00:04:36,551
The thirties are often the most expensive

140
00:04:36,551 --> 00:04:36,949
decade.

141
00:04:37,129 --> 00:04:40,505
The Department of Labor's national childcare price

142
00:04:40,505 --> 00:04:43,139
data shows full time childcare can cost

143
00:04:43,139 --> 00:04:45,772
many thousands of dollars per child per

144
00:04:45,772 --> 00:04:48,135
year, in some areas more than rent.

145
00:04:48,315 --> 00:04:49,391
Add a mortgage,

146
00:04:49,391 --> 00:04:52,332
a larger car and family health insurance,

147
00:04:52,332 --> 00:04:54,556
and it's easy to see why saving

148
00:04:54,556 --> 00:04:55,560
feels so hard.

149
00:04:55,910 --> 00:04:58,014
So the numbers are encouraging,

150
00:04:58,014 --> 00:04:59,915
but the decade is expensive.

151
00:05:00,095 --> 00:05:03,183
That's exactly why psychology matters so much.

152
00:05:03,363 --> 00:05:04,385
Angle two.

153
00:05:04,735 --> 00:05:05,652
Angle two.

154
00:05:05,832 --> 00:05:06,946
The psychology.

155
00:05:07,126 --> 00:05:08,593
Why panic is the enemy,

156
00:05:08,593 --> 00:05:11,271
and five mindset shifts for your thirties.

157
00:05:11,620 --> 00:05:13,218
When people feel behind,

158
00:05:13,218 --> 00:05:14,349
they often panic.

159
00:05:14,529 --> 00:05:17,640
And panic leads to three expensive mistakes.

160
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:19,251
The first is freezing:

161
00:05:19,251 --> 00:05:22,046
feeling so overwhelmed that you do nothing.

162
00:05:22,226 --> 00:05:23,902
The second is gambling:

163
00:05:23,902 --> 00:05:26,380
trying to catch up fast with risky

164
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:27,619
bets, hot stocks,

165
00:05:27,619 --> 00:05:30,388
or schemes that promise quick returns.

166
00:05:30,568 --> 00:05:32,960
The third is extreme restriction:

167
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:35,643
a budget so strict it collapses after

168
00:05:35,643 --> 00:05:36,512
a few weeks.

169
00:05:36,862 --> 00:05:37,594
Shift one.

170
00:05:37,774 --> 00:05:40,282
From behind, to on my own timeline.

171
00:05:40,462 --> 00:05:43,840
Comparison is especially intense in your thirties.

172
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:45,582
Friends buy houses,

173
00:05:45,582 --> 00:05:48,130
get promotions, post vacations.

174
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:50,177
But you don't know their debt,

175
00:05:50,177 --> 00:05:51,297
their family help,

176
00:05:51,297 --> 00:05:52,292
or their stress.

177
00:05:52,472 --> 00:05:54,642
The only comparison that helps is you

178
00:05:54,642 --> 00:05:55,932
versus you a year ago.

179
00:05:56,282 --> 00:05:57,176
Shift two.

180
00:05:57,356 --> 00:05:58,750
From guilt, to data.

181
00:05:58,930 --> 00:06:01,136
Many people avoid looking at their accounts

182
00:06:01,136 --> 00:06:02,471
because they feel ashamed.

183
00:06:02,651 --> 00:06:04,253
But shame doesn't build wealth.

184
00:06:04,433 --> 00:06:07,068
Looking at your numbers without judgment,

185
00:06:07,068 --> 00:06:09,318
like a doctor reading test results,

186
00:06:09,318 --> 00:06:11,503
turns a scary feeling into a plan.

187
00:06:11,854 --> 00:06:12,945
Shift three.

188
00:06:13,125 --> 00:06:15,319
From fast, to steady and strong.

189
00:06:15,499 --> 00:06:17,728
Catching up doesn't require high risk.

190
00:06:17,908 --> 00:06:20,591
It requires a higher savings rate and

191
00:06:20,591 --> 00:06:21,461
consistency.

192
00:06:21,641 --> 00:06:23,728
A boring plan that you follow for

193
00:06:23,728 --> 00:06:26,130
twenty five years will usually beat an

194
00:06:26,130 --> 00:06:28,407
exciting one you abandon after a bad

195
00:06:28,407 --> 00:06:28,723
year.

196
00:06:29,073 --> 00:06:29,897
Shift four.

197
00:06:30,077 --> 00:06:31,372
From either or,

198
00:06:31,372 --> 00:06:32,062
to both.

199
00:06:32,242 --> 00:06:33,333
In your thirties,

200
00:06:33,333 --> 00:06:36,027
money questions often feel like either or.

201
00:06:36,207 --> 00:06:37,716
Kids or retirement.

202
00:06:37,896 --> 00:06:39,545
House or investing.

203
00:06:39,725 --> 00:06:41,037
Fun or saving.

204
00:06:41,217 --> 00:06:42,642
But most of the time,

205
00:06:42,642 --> 00:06:44,270
the answer is a balance.

206
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:46,709
A good plan funds several goals at

207
00:06:46,709 --> 00:06:48,769
once, in the right proportions.

208
00:06:49,119 --> 00:06:50,188
And shift five.

209
00:06:50,368 --> 00:06:52,132
From someday, to systems.

210
00:06:52,312 --> 00:06:55,164
Busy lives in your thirties make willpower

211
00:06:55,164 --> 00:06:55,911
unreliable.

212
00:06:56,091 --> 00:06:58,877
Systems, like automatic contributions,

213
00:06:58,877 --> 00:07:02,028
automatic increases and automatic bill pay,

214
00:07:02,028 --> 00:07:04,300
keep working when you're tired,

215
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:05,693
busy or distracted.

216
00:07:05,873 --> 00:07:06,810
Set them up once,

217
00:07:06,810 --> 00:07:08,079
and let them carry you.

218
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:10,356
Here's what people often forget.

219
00:07:10,536 --> 00:07:13,134
Your thirties come with real advantages over

220
00:07:13,134 --> 00:07:13,961
your twenties.

221
00:07:14,141 --> 00:07:15,371
You usually earn more.

222
00:07:15,551 --> 00:07:17,038
You know yourself better,

223
00:07:17,038 --> 00:07:18,999
including your spending triggers.

224
00:07:19,179 --> 00:07:20,972
You may have a partner to share

225
00:07:20,972 --> 00:07:22,186
costs and goals with.

226
00:07:22,366 --> 00:07:24,546
And you've seen enough market ups and

227
00:07:24,546 --> 00:07:26,548
downs to stay calmer when the next

228
00:07:26,548 --> 00:07:27,196
drop comes.

229
00:07:27,546 --> 00:07:30,093
You also have more information about what

230
00:07:30,093 --> 00:07:31,273
you actually value.

231
00:07:31,453 --> 00:07:32,405
In your twenties,

232
00:07:32,405 --> 00:07:34,588
people often spend on things they think

233
00:07:34,588 --> 00:07:35,540
they should want.

234
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:36,722
In your thirties,

235
00:07:36,722 --> 00:07:38,666
many people find it easier to cut

236
00:07:38,666 --> 00:07:40,610
the things they don't care about,

237
00:07:40,610 --> 00:07:42,790
and spend more on what truly matters.

238
00:07:42,970 --> 00:07:45,137
That kind of clarity makes saving much

239
00:07:45,137 --> 00:07:45,536
easier.

240
00:07:45,886 --> 00:07:46,780
Angle three.

241
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:47,773
The playbook.

242
00:07:47,953 --> 00:07:49,832
Fifteen moves for your thirties,

243
00:07:49,832 --> 00:07:51,007
in a sensible order.

244
00:07:51,357 --> 00:07:52,030
Move one.

245
00:07:52,210 --> 00:07:53,487
Get your full picture.

246
00:07:53,837 --> 00:07:56,165
Gather every account in one place:

247
00:07:56,165 --> 00:07:59,795
checking, savings, retirement accounts from old jobs,

248
00:07:59,795 --> 00:08:01,987
debts, and their interest rates.

249
00:08:02,167 --> 00:08:03,504
Write down your net worth:

250
00:08:03,504 --> 00:08:05,150
what you own minus what you owe.

251
00:08:05,330 --> 00:08:06,654
It might be negative.

252
00:08:06,834 --> 00:08:07,600
That's okay.

253
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:09,197
It's your starting line.

254
00:08:09,547 --> 00:08:12,328
Many people in their thirties discover old

255
00:08:12,328 --> 00:08:14,713
401k accounts they'd half forgotten.

256
00:08:14,893 --> 00:08:17,355
Consolidating them into one IRA or your

257
00:08:17,355 --> 00:08:19,818
current plan can make your money easier

258
00:08:19,818 --> 00:08:21,712
to manage and help you see the

259
00:08:21,712 --> 00:08:23,101
whole picture clearly.

260
00:08:23,452 --> 00:08:24,438
Move two.

261
00:08:24,618 --> 00:08:26,511
Build a real emergency fund.

262
00:08:26,860 --> 00:08:28,598
With more responsibilities,

263
00:08:28,598 --> 00:08:31,365
your emergency fund matters more than ever.

264
00:08:31,545 --> 00:08:33,557
Aim for three to six months of

265
00:08:33,557 --> 00:08:36,373
essential expenses in a high yield savings

266
00:08:36,373 --> 00:08:36,909
account.

267
00:08:37,089 --> 00:08:38,446
If you have kids,

268
00:08:38,446 --> 00:08:40,839
a mortgage or a single income,

269
00:08:40,839 --> 00:08:42,116
lean toward six.

270
00:08:42,466 --> 00:08:43,941
Build it in stages.

271
00:08:44,121 --> 00:08:45,517
First one month,

272
00:08:45,517 --> 00:08:47,349
then three, then six.

273
00:08:47,529 --> 00:08:49,631
Keep contributing at least enough to get

274
00:08:49,631 --> 00:08:51,681
your employer match while you build it,

275
00:08:51,681 --> 00:08:53,206
so you don't miss free money.

276
00:08:53,555 --> 00:08:54,554
Move three.

277
00:08:54,734 --> 00:08:56,127
Clear high interest debt.

278
00:08:56,477 --> 00:08:58,923
Credit card debt at twenty percent or

279
00:08:58,923 --> 00:09:01,632
more makes catching up almost impossible.

280
00:09:01,812 --> 00:09:03,491
List your debts by rate,

281
00:09:03,491 --> 00:09:04,890
pay minimums on all,

282
00:09:04,890 --> 00:09:07,269
and attack the highest rate first.

283
00:09:07,449 --> 00:09:09,278
Every card you clear frees up money

284
00:09:09,278 --> 00:09:10,061
you can invest.

285
00:09:10,411 --> 00:09:12,022
If your credit is good,

286
00:09:12,022 --> 00:09:14,404
a balance transfer card or a lower

287
00:09:14,404 --> 00:09:16,365
